{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1533926-elevator-pitch-mage-the-quintessence-mage-in-space","id":1533926,"title":"Elevator Pitch - Mage: The Quintessence (Mage IN SPACE)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-04-09T20:14:02-07:00","id":1533926,"title":"Elevator Pitch - Mage: The Quintessence (Mage IN SPACE)","content":"So I was watching that Dark Side of the Moon/Eclipse animation contest runner up that has been making the rounds and I was hit with an idea I have to imagine dozens of people have already had: Mage in Space! Hell, the Revised Storyteller Handbook even had a version of it.<br /> <br /> Still, for a game that often has Moebius style artwork in its books the examples of Mage in Space I've seen have usually been relatively constrained. Maybe do the Biscocchian Coprosperity Sphere, or add Mage to Aeon/Trinity Continuum, etc. My thought though is if someone is going to do Mage in Space, then they need to go ALL OUT on the idea. This can serve as a contrast to the mainline series, where the authors generally implicitly insist that the primary play style should be gutter punk street mages, even when they say they want the game to be open to wilder Chronicles.<br /> <br /> Now I'm still working on my other book project, and if I'm going to do this right this one would likely be 3 times or more bigger and need brand new, commissioned art for it, so this is probably way out of my range to do as a solo project. Still, I'd like to work out an elevator pitch for it.<br /> <br /> Here's what I got so far:<ul><li>Title: Mage: The Quintessence</li> <li>Synopsis: Near the mid 21st Century, Earth and the Solar System become doomed, ironically not by human hands, but because of the other Night Folk in the cWoD settings having their respective Time of Judgement scenario happen simultaneously. Fleeing the Antediluvians, the Wyrm, etc., Various Awakened groups take as people as they can and flee to the stars. 500 to 1000 years later (haven't fully decided what the time jump should be), the Council of Nine Techno-Mystic Colonies and Technocratic Union of Planets butt into each other in space and the Ascension war begins anew. Throw in various aliens, the usual Nephandi/Marauder shenanigans, and a 2nd Disparate Alliance into the mix.</li> <li>Inspirations:<ul><li>The Incal/Jodoverse: Duh</li> <li>The Fifth Element: Also Duh</li> <li>Other Moebius works: Also also Duh</li> <li>L'Engle's Time Quintet: Alien worlds, space/time travel, microverses</li> <li>Eclipse Phase: Technocratic Union Colonies</li> <li>Cowboy Bebop: Lived in multicultural futurescapes</li> <li>Star Trek: Exploring new worlds, space diplomacy</li> <li>Aeon Flux: Pseudo religious scifi</li> <li>Xenogears/saga/blade: More Pseudo religious scifi, The Gnosis as an Alien threat. Alien vistas.</li> <li>Saga Frontier: Technological and Magical worlds co-existing in a broader universe.</li> <li>Gravity Rush: More Moebius like visuals, void monsters, umbral realms</li> <li>Trinity Continuum: Aeon - Storyteller derived Scifi</li> </ul></li> </ul>Thoughts would be appreciated.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5286-becksworth","name":"Becksworth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T02:08:56-07:00","id":1534019,"title":"","content":"Given the sheer vastness of space, the conflict between the Trads and the Technos should be low key on the surface. Look at the 19th century rivalry between Britain and Russia. It was cruel and vicious, but it was called &quot;The Game of Shadows&quot; for a reason. <br /> <br /> Also, the Desparate factions should be in deep space as well. They'd make good &quot;Planet of Hats&quot; fodder.<br /> <br /> The Changelings would make good &quot;Aliens&quot; of the Spock, Troi, Maya (Space 1999), Nyssa, The Doctor, types. For out there Aliens, try Umbrood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T06:26:20-07:00","id":1534022,"title":"","content":"If you want a re-emergence of the Ascension War, you need to have some kind of resource they are competing over. If each faction gets its own full earth/umbra combo there's not going to be much reason to fight for a long time, and maintaining the right cultures on each planet will be much more important than trying to manipulate the cultures on a different one. If, on the other hand, the planets are much thinner and weaker versions of earth (low on quintessence, shallow umbras, etc) then factions are going to need a lot of small and difficult to defend outposts.<br /> <br /> Also, the Technocracy is severely hindered in this scenario. A lot of their biggest advantages go away when the local reality doesn't back them up. They're going to have to lean heavily towards hypertech and will soon invoke Clarke's Law and become another form of Space Wizard. This is another reason to keep quintessence sources low as Primal Ventures will become a much needed leveler. <br /> <br /> I'll add one more inspiration: Alpha Centauri. Even though the game is centered around one planet, the different approaches and ideology driven conflict fit this idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T15:23:15-07:00","id":1534054,"title":"","content":"The Alpha Centauri idea is sound. Read the GURPS treatment and steal freely. <br /> <br /> Competition for resources drove conflicts among the European colonial empires from the 1550s to the 1940s. Ramnesis is suggesting resource wars. I'm adding in the possibilities of modeling the struggle on European colonial competition. <br /> <br /> Piracy in the Caribbean was very much driven by resource competition in Europe. The Technocrats can be Spain, the Trads can be France, England, and the Dutch. As the actual pirates try the Disperates. <br /> <br /> The game of Shadows (the Anglo-Russian struggle to dominate central Asia in the 19th century) with courtesy in the light and daggers in the shadows, is also a fine model.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T20:49:27-07:00","id":1534283,"title":"","content":"Okay, 3rd attempt at posting this. Going to break it up into multiple posts as I go so the forum software doesn't lose it all again...again...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1533926-elevator-pitch-mage-the-quintessence-mage-in-space?p=1534022#post1534022\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want a re-emergence of the Ascension War, you need to have some kind of resource they are competing over. If each faction gets its own full earth/umbra combo there's not going to be much reason to fight for a long time, and maintaining the right cultures on each planet will be much more important than trying to manipulate the cultures on a different one. If, on the other hand, the planets are much thinner and weaker versions of earth (low on quintessence, shallow umbras, etc) then factions are going to need a lot of small and difficult to defend outposts.</div> </div> </div> </div>My working idea is each star functions as a node providing power to sustain patterns in the system. However, Sol appears to be anomalous in how much quintessence it provided, and other star can't sustain nearly as much sentient life, civilization, and their associated consensus-es before pattern bleed and disembodiement become issues. This leads to the need expand, and the Union in particular stumbling upon Kha'vadi planets and beginning a whole new era of colonialism.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5286-becksworth","name":"Becksworth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T20:58:30-07:00","id":1534284,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=Ramnesis;n1534022]Also, the Technocracy is severely hindered in this scenario. A lot of their biggest advantages go away when the local reality doesn't back them up. They're going to have to lean heavily towards hypertech and will soon invoke Clarke's Law and become another form of Space Wizard. This is another reason to keep quintessence sources low as Primal Ventures will become a much needed leveler./QUOTE]<br /> <br /> I don't think the Technocracy is at that big of a disadvantage. They still have a unified paradigm easily shared with the masses, which even more a technomantic Council of Nine struggles with. The traditions will have bigger reality zones, sure, but still a fraction of the Unions total sphere of influence. I think the Technocratic Unions biggest issue is they'll likely also be butting heads with Aliens civilizations on a different front from the Traditions.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5286-becksworth","name":"Becksworth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T21:03:01-07:00","id":1534285,"title":"","content":"Been thinking of how the Traditions would differ in a millenium, and here's what I came up with so far:<ul><li>The Council of Nine Techno-Mystic Colonies<ul><li>Akashayana - ?</li> <li>Celestial Chorus - Like Bene Gesserits maybe? Perhaps musical motifs also become more literal with time.</li> <li>The Ethernauts - Space Jockeys, Explorers, Racers, and general Grease Monkeys of the Traditions. What came of the Society of Ether. When everyone does some form of weird science, their two fisted pulp sensibilities and love of adventure became what defined them.</li> <li>Karma Proximus - What came of the Chakravanti when they had to single handedly police an entire colony for a millenium. Ergo Proxy meets Judge Dread</li> <li>Kha'vadi - Still a collection of smaller \u201cfirst nation\u201d colonies that there before everyone else due to the Kha'vadi emphasis of using the Umbra to transport small groups of settlers to various places. Some old Earth cultures still exist, but many settlements have developed their own unique cultures.</li> <li>Order of Hermes - Adapt surprisingly well thanks to technomancer houses. Their practices are more magitech artificery and enochian based reality hacking now. Solar sail ships (they can control solar winds after all)</li> <li>The Principality of Whyq - Biotech Feudal society ruled by the moon princess and sun prince. What comes of the Verbena after a millenium free from religious persecution and being in charge.</li> <li>Sahajiya - ? Part of me wants to completely abandon the sex drugs &amp; rock n roll vibe, and the other part wants to go full Heavy Metal Magazine with them.</li> <li>The Virtual Ones - Digital spirits with avatars. Manifest awakened and must find an artificial body to possess to keep their avatar from disconnecting. Believed to be the reincarnated Virtual Adepts who opted to upload themselves to the Digital Web.</li> </ul></li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5286-becksworth","name":"Becksworth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T21:05:22-07:00","id":1534286,"title":"","content":"All that said, I was reviewing the Storyteller Vault rules and I think historical alt settings are allowed but not speculative future ones, so yeah this will probably remain an elevator pitch for the time being...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5286-becksworth","name":"Becksworth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T14:38:16-07:00","id":1534307,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=Becksworth;n1534284]<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1533926-elevator-pitch-mage-the-quintessence-mage-in-space?p=1534022#post1534022\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, the Technocracy is severely hindered in this scenario. A lot of their biggest advantages go away when the local reality doesn't back them up. They're going to have to lean heavily towards hypertech and will soon invoke Clarke's Law and become another form of Space Wizard. This is another reason to keep quintessence sources low as Primal Ventures will become a much needed leveler./QUOTE]<br /> <br /> I don't think the Technocracy is at that big of a disadvantage. They still have a unified paradigm easily shared with the masses, which even more a technomantic Council of Nine struggles with. The traditions will have bigger reality zones, sure, but still a fraction of the Unions total sphere of influence. I think the Technocratic Unions biggest issue is they'll likely also be butting heads with Aliens civilizations on a different front from the Traditions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the Technocracy's biggest disadvantage is they can't breathe in the Deep Umbra/Universe. Whether that's still a restriction in a thousand years is the question. <br /> <br /> By the same reasoning, though, I would expect the Traditions to learn at least a little from the Ascension War on Earth. While they may still favor paradigms that rest on special enlightenment or individual excellence, I don't think they'll leave the populace of their domains in the dark. Education (and indoctrination, depending on how dark you want to go) are too powerful and important to maintaining reality zones. The Technocracy will find it much harder to spread their paradigm in a society already well educated in that of another.<br /> <br /> Of course it is more interesting if mages only start each new outpost and the societies grow beyond their full control as they get more populated. Then you have the possibility of strange bleedover between paradigms and strange phenomenon: a sort of mystical wild west.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T20:49:17-07:00","id":1534320,"title":"","content":"What kind of technology and magic that the Consensus permits as a baseline is going to be pretty important to the kind of setting that you are describing. Just off of the top of my head, things change drastically if the Awakened are the only ones capable of interstellar travel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T12:39:56-07:00","id":1534359,"title":"","content":"I could see the Hermetics, the Virtual Adepts, and the Etherites, teaming up with the Children of Knowledge to improve pedagogy. If the Akashayama get in on it, maybe Psionic Numina are fairly common. <br /> <br /> Maybe the Technos have the cyborgs, bio enhancements, and the higher tech level. The Trad worlds have lots of Psions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-17T12:54:13-07:00","id":1534362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1533926-elevator-pitch-mage-the-quintessence-mage-in-space?p=1534359#post1534359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I could see the Hermetics, the Virtual Adepts, and the Etherites, teaming up with the Children of Knowledge to improve pedagogy. If the Akashayama get in on it, maybe Psionic Numina are fairly common.<br /> <br /> Maybe the Technos have the cyborgs, bio enhancements, and the higher tech level. The Trad worlds have lots of Psions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see a fair deal of psychics being among the more technomantic paradigms as well. The Technocracy would probably loosen some of their restrictions on psi powers, both for practical and ideological reasons.<br /> <br /> You are probably right in that the Traditions would have more psychics, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-21T21:05:30-07:00","id":1534538,"title":"","content":"You should think a bit about the Cosmology of the setting. Mage features an Earth-centric cosmology where even in the 21st century, you run into a barrier if you try to climb into a rocket ship and fly out of the Solar System. For an interstellar system, I could see recontextualizing this to being how <i>every</i> star system is: the Deep Umbra is interstellar space, and every developed star system exists inside a bubble of Conventional Space.<br /> <br /> Less developed systems limit their bubbles of Conventional Space to planetary orbit, the way the Cosmology was described in the earlier editions of Mage; or even just to the edge of the atmosphere, as was the case back in the Sorcerers Crusade. Worlds that haven't been settled yet may exist within the Umbra, without <i>any</i> Conventional Space. The descendants of the Void Engineers are looking to change that, as there are considerable benefits to operating within Conventional Space, such as not having to protect yourself against Void Adaptation; but it's a big task. <br /> <br /> The Void Engineers Convention Book argues that if you don't want to Void Adapt, you need to establish some sort of connection to an Earth Node. Fair enough, as far as it goes; but then, the Syndicate Convention Book introduces Primal Ventures, where organized human activity can function like a Node. I could see the crew of a starship functioning that way, with even a small ship's crew being adequate to provide enough of a Node to maintain an Earthlike environment on board the ship; though I'd recommend that such crew-supported Voidships maintain regular contact with each other and with human colonies, or else they risk a sort of gradual reality drift. <br /> <br /> You might consider introducing something like Stargate SG-1 as a way to let humanity spread to the stars without making them dependent on starships to do it. I think that most of the Traditions would appreciate this option, as the Stargates wouldn't necessarily have to be technological. I could see any world connected to the Stargate network essentially sharing the same Conventional Space, even if divided among distant \u201cbubbles\u201d. <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> But then there are the <i>actual</i> aliens, who don't come from Earth <i>at all</i>, and who come from alien environments. I'd use the STV Changeling product <a href=\"https://preview.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/314491/Changeling-Countless-Dreams\" target=\"_blank\">Countless Dreams</a> as the basis for this: you have Kites (Abyssal or Celestial aliens that live either in the sky or the depths of the ocean; good for populating gas giants and waterworlds), Silicates (Aidoneus or Digital entities that are either machines or residents of the Digital Web), Forgotten (Ancestors and Explorers who represent animalistic aliens or inhabitants of interplanetary space), and Oppies (inorganic aliens).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-29T14:44:21-07:00","id":1559104,"title":"","content":"Population has a relationship both to Paradox and to the difficulty of casting. <br /> <br /> According to one of the Story teller guides difficulty of preforming magic increases when a planet's Population hits certain milestones. Populations of five million, fifty million, and three hundred million all corespond to increasing casting difficulties. Thus a recently settled frontier world might attract Mages wanting freedom from Paradox (which doesn't seem to exist below populations of four hundred million) and reduced casting difficulties. <br /> <br /> Paradox wasn't evenly spread over the planet Earth even in the early 21st century. At least according to cannon sources. So a world with five settled areas, each with a population of around one million and those areas are, a Void Engineer scout base, an Hermetic University, and Etherite Utopian settlement, a Wu Lung cultural recreation settlement, and a Ngoma agricultural settlement, might have different rules of reality in each area. Especially if the areas are far apart and densely settled. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}